[38;5;231m⏺[39m Last fork before I put a design together: [1mwhat happens on a hostname that isn't in the map[0m — a new preview URL, a
  teammate's machine, a typo.

  - [1mFall back to development + console warning[0m — unknown host gets the dev endpoint and a visible warning. Buys: an
    unregistered host can never silently talk to production. Costs: deploying to a new prod hostname appears to "work"
    while quietly hitting dev until someone adds the entry.
  - [1mFall back to production[0m — unknown host gets the prod endpoint. Buys: a new deploy target works immediately with no
    map edit. Costs: a typo'd or local hostname points at real production data, which is the expensive direction to be
    wrong in.
  - [1mThrow on unknown host[0m — refuse to load without a registered entry. Buys: impossible to be on the wrong endpoint
    unknowingly. Costs: the page hard-breaks on every unlisted host, including ad-hoc previews.

  [1mMy recommendation: fall back to development with a console warning.[0m Both failure modes are real, but "silently pointed
  at dev" is a bug you notice and fix, while "silently pointed at prod" can be a data incident. Throwing is defensible,
  and it's a one-line change from the fallback version if you later want it.
